body:
`getUuid()` can still throw `IllegalArgumentException` when the JDBC driver returns a `byte[]` that isn’t 16 bytes long (since it unconditionally calls `bytesToUuid`). This contradicts the method’s Javadoc (“return null … on unexpected type”) and may still reproduce the original 36-byte UUID failure if the driver exposes UUIDs as text bytes. Consider handling common UUID representations explicitly (e.g., `String`/`CharSequence`, or `byte[]` length 36 parsed as a UUID string), or use `resultSet.getObject(columnName, UUID.class)` with a safe fallback; if conversion fails, log/throw a clear `SQLException` rather than bubbling an `IllegalArgumentException` from a utility method.
```suggestion
        if (uuidObject == null) {
            return null;
        }
        if (uuidObject instanceof UUID uuid) {
            return uuid;
        }
        if (uuidObject instanceof byte[] uuidBytes) {
            if (uuidBytes.length == 16) {
                return bytesToUuid(uuidBytes);
            }

            String uuidString = new String(uuidBytes, java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets.UTF_8).trim();
            try {
                return UUID.fromString(uuidString);
            } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
                throw new SQLException(
                        "Invalid UUID value in column '" + columnName + "': expected 16-byte binary UUID or UUID string bytes, but received byte[" + uuidBytes.length + "]",
                        e
                );
            }
        }
        if (uuidObject instanceof CharSequence uuidChars) {
            try {
                return UUID.fromString(uuidChars.toString().trim());
            } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
                throw new SQLException(
                        "Invalid UUID value in column '" + columnName + "': '" + uuidChars + "'",
                        e
                );
            }
        }
